## Modules

Modern code uses ES modules: one file, one module, with explicit imports and exports.

~~~tsx
// price.ts
export function formatPrice(amount: number, currency = "USD") {
  return new Intl.NumberFormat("en-GB", { style: "currency", currency }).format(amount);
}

// cart.ts
import { formatPrice } from "./price";
~~~

Intl.NumberFormat and Intl.DateTimeFormat handle currencies, dates and plurals for every locale, which matters on international products.

## Working with data

~~~tsx
const jobs = [
  { title: "Data engineer", country: "DE", salary: 72000 },
  { title: "SRE", country: "US", salary: 150000 },
  { title: "Analyst", country: "IN", salary: 18000 },
];

const europe = jobs.filter((j) => ["DE", "NL", "FR"].includes(j.country));
const titles = jobs.map((j) => j.title);
const total = jobs.reduce((sum, j) => sum + j.salary, 0);
const { title, ...rest } = jobs[0];          // destructuring and rest
const updated = { ...jobs[0], salary: 75000 }; // copy with a change
~~~

React depends on **immutability**: create new arrays and objects rather than changing existing ones, so React can detect changes by reference.

## Asynchronous code

~~~tsx
async function loadJobs(country: string) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/jobs?country=${encodeURIComponent(country)}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

const [jobs, courses] = await Promise.all([loadJobs("DE"), fetch("/api/courses").then((r) => r.json())]);
~~~

- **Promise.all** runs independent requests in parallel; awaiting them one by one adds their latencies together.
- Always check res.ok: fetch only rejects on network failure, not on 404 or 500.

## Optional chaining and nullish coalescing

user?.address?.city returns undefined instead of throwing, and count ?? 0 falls back only for null or undefined, unlike count || 0, which also replaces 0.

## Why teams insist on it

TypeScript catches whole classes of bugs before code runs, documents intent, and makes large refactors safe. Most React job descriptions now expect it.

## Types and interfaces

~~~tsx
type Currency = "USD" | "EUR" | "GBP" | "INR";

type Job = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  salary: { min: number; max: number; currency: Currency };
  remote: boolean;
  tags?: string[];          // optional
};
~~~

Union types such as Currency restrict values to a known set, so a typo like "UDS" fails at compile time.

## Narrowing

~~~tsx
type Result = { ok: true; data: Job[] } | { ok: false; error: string };

function render(result: Result) {
  if (result.ok) {
    return result.data.length;   // TypeScript knows data exists here
  }
  return result.error;           // and error exists here
}
~~~

This **discriminated union** pattern models success and failure explicitly and is common in API and form handling.

## Generics

~~~tsx
function first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}
const job = first(jobs);          // inferred as Job | undefined
~~~

## Utility types

| Type | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Partial<Job> | Every property optional |
| Pick<Job, "id" \| "title"> | Only the listed properties |
| Omit<Job, "salary"> | Everything except salary |
| Record<Currency, number> | An object keyed by every currency |

## Settings worth having

Enable "strict": true in tsconfig.json. Avoid any; use unknown for data from outside, such as JSON responses, and validate it with a schema library such as Zod before trusting it.

## Components are functions

A React component is a function that takes **props** and returns JSX describing the interface.

~~~tsx
type JobCardProps = { title: string; company: string; remote: boolean; onSave: () => void };

export function JobCard({ title, company, remote, onSave }: JobCardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="card">
      <h3>{title}</h3>
      <p>{company}{remote && " · Remote"}</p>
      <button type="button" onClick={onSave}>Save job</button>
    </article>
  );
}
~~~

Props flow down; events flow up through callbacks such as onSave.

## State

~~~tsx
import { useState } from "react";

export function SkillFilter({ skills }: { skills: string[] }) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string[]>([]);

  function toggle(skill: string) {
    setSelected((prev) => (prev.includes(skill) ? prev.filter((s) => s !== skill) : [...prev, skill]));
  }

  return (
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Skills</legend>
      {skills.map((skill) => (
        <label key={skill}>
          <input type="checkbox" checked={selected.includes(skill)} onChange={() => toggle(skill)} />
          {skill}
        </label>
      ))}
    </fieldset>
  );
}
~~~

Key rules:

- **Never mutate state.** Return new arrays and objects.
- Use the **updater form** (prev => ...) when the next state depends on the previous one.
- **Keys** must be stable and unique among siblings. Use IDs, not array indexes, for lists that can reorder.

## Where state lives

Keep state as low as possible and **lift it up** to the closest common parent when two components need it. Derive values during render instead of storing them: if you have a list and a filter, compute the filtered list each render rather than keeping a second copy in state.

## Hooks rules

Call hooks only at the top level of components or custom hooks, never inside conditions or loops, so React can match each call to its state between renders.

## You might not need an effect

useEffect synchronises a component with something **outside React**: a subscription, a timer, a browser API. It is not for transforming data or reacting to events.

~~~tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setNow(Date.now()), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);   // cleanup on unmount or before re-running
}, []);
~~~

Common mistakes:

- Computing derived state in an effect, causing an extra render. Compute it during render instead, or use useMemo if it is expensive.
- Missing dependencies, which leaves stale values. Keep the eslint react-hooks rules switched on.
- Fetching in effects without handling race conditions. In Next.js, fetch on the server instead, or use a library such as TanStack Query.

## Forms in React 19

React 19 supports actions on forms and the useActionState hook, which tracks the result of a submission and whether it is pending.

~~~tsx
"use client";
import { useActionState } from "react";

export function ApplyForm({ apply }: { apply: (prev: string | null, data: FormData) => Promise<string | null> }) {
  const [error, formAction, pending] = useActionState(apply, null);
  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <label htmlFor="cover">Cover note</label>
      <textarea id="cover" name="cover" required minLength={50} />
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
      <button disabled={pending}>{pending ? "Sending..." : "Apply"}</button>
    </form>
  );
}
~~~

## Custom hooks

Extract reusable stateful logic into a function whose name starts with use:

~~~tsx
function useDebounced<T>(value: T, delay = 300) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;
}
~~~

A debounced search box then calls the API only when the user pauses typing.

## Server components by default

Every component in the App Router is a **server component** unless the file starts with "use client". Server components:

- Run only on the server, so they can read databases and secrets directly.
- Send no JavaScript for themselves to the browser.
- Can be async and await data.

Add "use client" only where you need state, effects, event handlers or browser APIs, and push it to the **leaves** of the tree: a page can stay on the server while a small filter panel is a client component.

## Fetching data

~~~tsx
// app/jobs/page.tsx (server component)
export default async function JobsPage({ searchParams }: { searchParams: Promise<{ country?: string }> }) {
  const { country } = await searchParams;
  const jobs = await db.jobs.findMany({ where: { country }, take: 20 });
  return <JobList jobs={jobs} />;
}
~~~

## Mutating with Server Actions

~~~tsx
// app/jobs/actions.ts
"use server";
import { revalidatePath } from "next/cache";

export async function saveJob(formData: FormData) {
  const user = await requireUser();                 // always re-check auth on the server
  const jobId = String(formData.get("jobId") ?? "");
  await db.savedJobs.create({ userId: user.id, jobId });
  revalidatePath("/jobs/saved");
}
~~~

Server Actions are public HTTP endpoints under the hood. **Validate input and check authorisation inside every action**, exactly as you would in an API route.

## Caching

- In Next.js 15, fetch requests and GET route handlers are **not cached by default**; opt in where data can be shared.
- Use revalidatePath or revalidateTag after mutations so pages show fresh data.
- Wrap slow parts of a page in Suspense to stream the rest immediately.

## Passing data across the boundary

Props passed from server to client components must be serialisable: plain objects, arrays, strings, numbers, dates. Functions cannot be passed, except Server Actions.

## What to test

Test behaviour the user sees, not implementation details. A good mix:

- **Unit tests** for pure functions such as price formatting or skill matching.
- **Component tests** with React Testing Library for interactive pieces.
- **End-to-end tests** with Playwright for critical journeys such as sign-in, search and apply.

## A component test with Vitest

~~~tsx
import { render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
import userEvent from "@testing-library/user-event";
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { SkillFilter } from "./skill-filter";

describe("SkillFilter", () => {
  it("toggles a skill when its checkbox is clicked", async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup();
    render(<SkillFilter skills={["React", "TypeScript"]} />);

    const react = screen.getByRole("checkbox", { name: "React" });
    expect(react).not.toBeChecked();
    await user.click(react);
    expect(react).toBeChecked();
  });
});
~~~

## Query priorities

Prefer queries that mirror how people and assistive technology find elements:

1. getByRole with an accessible name.
2. getByLabelText for form fields.
3. getByText for non-interactive content.
4. getByTestId only as a last resort.

If you cannot find an element by role and name, real users of screen readers probably cannot either, so the test has found an accessibility bug.

## Async server components

Async server components are best covered by end-to-end tests. Keep their data-loading logic in plain functions that you can unit test directly.

## In the pipeline

Run type checks, ESLint and tests on every pull request, and Playwright against a preview deployment before merging to main.

## Modelling with sealed types

Sealed interfaces restrict which classes can implement them, so the compiler knows every case.

~~~java
public sealed interface PaymentResult permits Approved, Declined, NeedsReview {}
public record Approved(String reference) implements PaymentResult {}
public record Declined(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}
public record NeedsReview(String caseId) implements PaymentResult {}

String describe(PaymentResult result) {
    return switch (result) {
        case Approved a -> "Approved: " + a.reference();
        case Declined d -> "Declined: " + d.reason();
        case NeedsReview n -> "Under review: " + n.caseId();
    };
}
~~~

There is no default branch: if someone adds a new result type, every switch that misses it fails to compile. This is safer than a chain of if statements.

## Concurrency basics

- Prefer **immutable objects** and thread-safe collections such as ConcurrentHashMap.
- Use an **ExecutorService** rather than creating threads by hand.
- Use **CompletableFuture** to compose asynchronous work.

## Virtual threads (Java 21)

Platform threads map one-to-one to operating system threads and are expensive, which is why thread pools exist. **Virtual threads** are lightweight threads managed by the JVM: you can run hundreds of thousands, and a blocking call simply parks the virtual thread.

~~~java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    List<Future<Quote>> quotes = suppliers.stream()
        .map(s -> executor.submit(() -> s.fetchQuote(order)))
        .toList();
}
~~~

In Spring Boot 3.2 and later, setting spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true serves each request on a virtual thread. This suits I/O-heavy services that call databases and other APIs, letting simple blocking code scale without reactive programming.

## Watch out for

Virtual threads do not make CPU-bound work faster, and long synchronized blocks around blocking I/O can pin the carrier thread in older JDKs. Profile before and after.

## A REST controller

~~~java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/applications")
public class ApplicationController {
    private final ApplicationService service;

    public ApplicationController(ApplicationService service) { this.service = service; }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<ApplicationResponse> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateApplicationRequest request) {
        var created = service.apply(request);
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/v1/applications/" + created.id())).body(created);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ApplicationResponse get(@PathVariable UUID id) {
        return service.find(id);
    }
}

public record CreateApplicationRequest(
    @NotNull UUID jobId,
    @NotBlank @Size(max = 2000) String coverNote,
    @Email String contactEmail) {}
~~~

## HTTP conventions

| Action | Method | Success status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Create | POST | 201 Created with a Location header |
| Read | GET | 200 OK |
| Replace or update | PUT or PATCH | 200 OK or 204 No Content |
| Delete | DELETE | 204 No Content |
| Invalid input | Any | 400 Bad Request |
| Not found | Any | 404 Not Found |
| Business rule conflict | Any | 409 Conflict |

Use plural nouns for resources, version the API in the path or a header, and paginate collections.

## Consistent errors with Problem Details

RFC 9457 defines a standard JSON error format, and Spring supports it through ProblemDetail.

~~~java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class ApiErrors {
    @ExceptionHandler(JobClosedException.class)
    ProblemDetail jobClosed(JobClosedException ex) {
        var pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, ex.getMessage());
        pd.setTitle("Job is closed");
        pd.setType(URI.create("https://api.example.com/problems/job-closed"));
        return pd;
    }
}
~~~

Set spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true so framework errors, such as validation failures, use the same format. Never return stack traces to clients; log them with a correlation ID instead.

## Entities and repositories

~~~java
@Entity
@Table(name = "applications")
public class Application {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private UUID id;
    @Column(nullable = false) private UUID jobId;
    @Column(nullable = false) private UUID candidateId;
    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) private Status status = Status.SUBMITTED;
    @Version private long version;
    // getters, domain methods
}

public interface ApplicationRepository extends JpaRepository<Application, UUID> {
    List<Application> findByCandidateIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc(UUID candidateId);
    boolean existsByJobIdAndCandidateId(UUID jobId, UUID candidateId);
}
~~~

Spring Data derives queries from method names. Use @Query with JPQL for anything more complex. @Version enables **optimistic locking**: if two requests update the same row, the second fails instead of silently overwriting.

## Transactions

~~~java
@Transactional
public ApplicationResponse apply(CreateApplicationRequest req, UUID candidateId) {
    if (applications.existsByJobIdAndCandidateId(req.jobId(), candidateId)) {
        throw new DuplicateApplicationException();
    }
    var saved = applications.save(new Application(req.jobId(), candidateId));
    return ApplicationResponse.from(saved);
}
~~~

- Put @Transactional on **service** methods, not controllers.
- Use @Transactional(readOnly = true) for queries.
- Calls within the same class bypass the proxy, so the annotation has no effect on self-invocation.

## The N+1 problem

Loading 50 applications and then lazily loading each job runs 51 queries. Fix it with a fetch join, an @EntityGraph or a DTO projection, and turn on SQL logging in development to spot it.

## Schema migrations with Flyway

~~~sql
-- src/main/resources/db/migration/V3__add_application_status.sql
ALTER TABLE applications ADD COLUMN status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'SUBMITTED';
CREATE INDEX idx_applications_candidate ON applications (candidate_id, created_at DESC);
~~~

Flyway applies versioned scripts in order at start-up and records them in a history table. Never edit a migration that has run in a shared environment; add a new one. Set spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate so Hibernate checks the schema but never changes it.

## The testing pyramid for services

- **Unit tests** for domain logic, with plain JUnit 5 and Mockito. Fast and plentiful.
- **Slice tests** such as @WebMvcTest (controllers only) and @DataJpaTest (repositories only).
- **Integration tests** with @SpringBootTest against real dependencies in containers.
- **Contract tests** between services, for example with Spring Cloud Contract or Pact.

## A controller slice test

~~~java
@WebMvcTest(ApplicationController.class)
class ApplicationControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
    @MockitoBean ApplicationService service;

    @Test
    void rejectsBlankCoverNote() throws Exception {
        mvc.perform(post("/api/v1/applications")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("""
                    {"jobId":"7f1c2a8e-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","coverNote":""}
                    """))
           .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
           .andExpect(jsonPath("$.title").value("Bad Request"));
    }
}
~~~

## Real databases with Testcontainers

In-memory databases such as H2 behave differently from PostgreSQL, so tests pass while production fails. Testcontainers starts a real database in Docker for the test run.

~~~java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class ApplicationFlowIT {
    @Container
    @ServiceConnection
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> db = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine");

    @Autowired ApplicationService service;

    @Test
    void preventsDuplicateApplications() {
        var request = new CreateApplicationRequest(JOB_ID, "I have five years of Spring experience.", null);
        service.apply(request, CANDIDATE_ID);
        assertThrows(DuplicateApplicationException.class, () -> service.apply(request, CANDIDATE_ID));
    }
}
~~~

@ServiceConnection (Spring Boot 3.1 and later) wires the container's connection details automatically, and Flyway migrations run against it, so the test also proves your migrations work.

## Habits

Name tests after behaviour, keep each test independent, and run the full suite in CI on every pull request.

## Start with the domain

Microservices are a way to let teams work and deploy independently. They are not free: you trade in-process calls for networks, transactions for eventual consistency, and one deployment for many. A well-structured **modular monolith** is often the right first step.

When you do split, follow **bounded contexts** from domain-driven design: areas where a term has one clear meaning. In a talent platform, "Jobs", "Applications", "Learning" and "Billing" are natural candidates, each owning its own data.

## Rules of thumb

- **Each service owns its database.** Other services never query it directly.
- **Design for failure.** Every remote call can be slow or fail.
- **Keep APIs backward compatible.** Add fields; do not rename or remove them without a versioned transition.

## Synchronous calls

Use HTTP when the caller needs an immediate answer. Spring's declarative HTTP interfaces keep client code small:

~~~java
@HttpExchange("/api/v1/jobs")
public interface JobClient {
    @GetExchange("/{id}")
    JobSummary get(@PathVariable UUID id);
}
~~~

## Asynchronous events

Use events when other services need to react but the caller does not need to wait. Publish an ApplicationSubmitted event to **Apache Kafka** or RabbitMQ; notifications, analytics and the recruiter dashboard consume it independently.

~~~java
@Transactional
public void submit(Application app) {
    applications.save(app);
    outbox.save(OutboxEvent.of("ApplicationSubmitted", app.id()));
}
~~~

The **transactional outbox** pattern writes the event to a table in the same transaction as the business change; a relay (for example Debezium) then publishes it. This avoids the dual-write problem where the database commits but the message is lost.

## Sagas

Business processes that span services use **sagas**: a sequence of local transactions with compensating actions, coordinated by events (choreography) or a central orchestrator.
